course: dictionary
lesson: 9890
title: The taka, money of Bangladesh
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A taka is money. It is the currency of Bangladesh, a country in South Asia, so people there pay for things with taka, just as we pay with pounds in Britain.
explain: One taka is made of 100 poisha. Prices may be shown with the symbol ৳, and banks and exchange tables use the code BDT for the taka.
explain: When you see «fifty taka» in a shopping problem, read the words around it. The price tells you it is money, and «Bangladeshi taka» tells you which country's money it is.
fact: A taka is the money of Bangladesh.
fact: One taka is worth 100 poisha.
fact: The symbol for the taka is ৳, and its code is BDT.
fact: In Bengali, the word «taka» also simply means money.
fact: Currency codes like BDT help banks and travellers compare money from different countries.
exercise: «The sweets cost fifty taka.» What is the taka? | A kind of sweet | A bus ticket | A town in Bangladesh | *The money of Bangladesh
exercise: How many poisha make one taka? | 1000 | 10 | 50 | *100
exercise: Which code do banks use for the taka? | BGD | *BDT | ৳ | TKA
test: «A pen costs five taka and a book costs one hundred taka.» Which costs more? | *The book | There is not enough information | They cost the same | The pen
test: The symbol ৳ shows which money? | The pound | The euro | *The taka | The rupee
exam: «A bag costs 250 taka and you pay with 300 taka.» How much change do you get? | 550 taka | 500 taka | 30 taka | *50 taka
